<h2>Key Finding #1: Review Recency Beats Review Count</h2>
<p><strong>The data:</strong> Review recency (days since most recent review) showed a <strong>-0.41 correlation</strong> with ranking position. Review count showed only <strong>-0.22</strong>.</p>
<p>Here's what that means in plain terms: a contractor whose most recent review is from yesterday is more likely to rank well than a contractor with 500 reviews whose most recent one is from six months ago.</p>
<p>This finding aligns with the <a href="https://whitespark.ca/blog/local-search-ranking-factors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Whitespark 2026 Local Search Ranking Factors Survey</a>, where review recency jumped from #20 in importance (2023) to a top-5 position. Darren Shaw, founder of Whitespark, noted that "the moment you stop getting new reviews, you're going to see your local rankings start to slip — even if your total count is massive."</p>
