<p><strong>The pattern is unmistakable.</strong> Top-ranked profiles average a fresh review every 8-14 days. Profiles ranking 11-20 go more than a month between reviews on average.</p>
<h3>Why This Matters More for Contractors</h3>
<p>Contractors face a unique challenge: most customers only need their services once every 5-15 years (roof replacement, HVAC installation). That natural cycle makes consistent review generation harder than for restaurants or salons.</p>
<p>The contractors who cracked this code did three things differently:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>They asked immediately.</strong> The highest-velocity profiles sent review requests within 24 hours of job completion — while the positive experience was fresh.</li>
<li><strong>They asked everyone.</strong> Not just the homeowner who paid the bill. They asked property managers, real estate agents, insurance adjusters — anyone who interacted with their work.</li>
<li><strong>They used multiple touchpoints.</strong> Text, email, and in-person requests. One plumbing contractor in our sample attributed 60% of their monthly reviews to a simple text message sent 2 hours after job completion.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Set up an automated review request system that triggers within 24 hours of job completion. Target 4-8 new reviews per month minimum. If your total review count is high but your recency is poor, your rankings are at risk regardless of your historical volume.</p>
