<p>Profiles in position 1 average <strong>3x more photos</strong> than profiles in positions 11-20.</p>
<h3>Why Photos Correlate So Strongly</h3>
<p>We believe this correlation works through multiple indirect mechanisms:</p>
<p><strong>1. Behavioral signals.</strong> Google's own data shows listings with photos receive 42% more direction requests and 35% more website clicks. These engagement metrics feed back into ranking algorithms.</p>
<p><strong>2. Vision AI analysis.</strong> Google uses computer vision to analyze photo content. A roofing contractor uploading photos of shingle installations, gutter work, and storm damage helps Google understand what services they offer — improving relevance matching.</p>
<p><strong>3. Profile completeness signal.</strong> Photo count is a proxy for overall profile maintenance. Contractors who upload 100+ photos are almost certainly also responding to reviews, updating hours, and posting regularly.</p>
<p><strong>4. Trust and click-through rate.</strong> In the local pack, profiles with rich photo galleries stand out visually. Higher CTR from search results sends positive behavioral signals.</p>
<h3>The Contractor Photo Strategy</h3>
<p>The highest-photo-count profiles in our sample followed a consistent pattern:</p>
<ul class="bullet-list">
<li><strong>Before/after photos</strong> (40% of uploads): Kitchen remodels, roof replacements, HVAC installations</li>
<li><strong>Team photos</strong> (20%): Crews on-site, uniformed technicians, company vehicles</li>
<li><strong>Equipment/vehicle photos</strong> (15%): Branded trucks, specialized tools, safety gear</li>
<li><strong>Project process photos</strong> (15%): Mid-job progress shots, detail work, cleanup</li>
<li><strong>Office/facility photos</strong> (10%): Showroom, warehouse, dispatch center</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Critical insight:</strong> The top-performing profiles added photos <strong>consistently</strong>, not in bulk. Profiles that uploaded 50 photos in one day and then went silent performed worse than profiles that added 2-3 photos per week ongoing.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Upload a minimum of 60 photos across all categories. Then add 2-3 new photos weekly. Use original images — not stock photos. Geotag images to your service area before upload (while Google strips EXIF data, the practice ensures your photo library is location-relevant).</p>
