</ul>
<h3>The Category Trap</h3>
<p>We found a common pattern among lower-ranked profiles: contractors selecting overly broad categories in an attempt to rank for more queries. A roofing company selecting "Construction company" as their primary category hoping to also capture general construction searches. An electrician selecting "Home improvement" to cast a wider net.</p>
<p>This backfires. Google's algorithm uses primary category as a core relevance signal. When someone searches "roofing contractor," Google prioritizes profiles categorized as "Roofing contractor" — not profiles that happen to offer roofing among 12 other services.</p>
<h3>The Secondary Category Strategy</h3>
<p>Here's where it gets interesting. Profiles with <strong>3-4 well-chosen secondary categories</strong> outperformed profiles with either zero secondaries or 8+ secondaries. This suggests a "Goldilocks zone" — enough to signal breadth, not so many that relevance gets diluted.</p>
