<p><strong>The threshold effect is clear.</strong> Posting 3-4 times per month moves the needle. Posting 5-8 times per month moves it more. But the biggest jump is from "never" to "sometimes" — profiles posting even 1-2 times per month dramatically outperformed inactive profiles.</p>
<h3>Why Posts Matter (Beyond the Obvious)</h3>
<p>GBP posts expire after 7 days. A profile that hasn't posted in a month shows nothing in the "Updates" section of their Knowledge Panel. This creates a perception of inactivity — both for Google and for potential customers.</p>
<p>But there's a deeper mechanism at work. Posts generate direct engagement (clicks, calls, direction requests) that feed behavioral signals. They also allow keyword reinforcement — a roofing contractor posting about "hail damage repair in Plano" signals relevance for that specific query.</p>
<h3>What the Top Posters Actually Share</h3>
<p>We analyzed the content of posts from the highest-frequency profiles. The pattern:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>40% project updates:</strong> "Just completed a full roof replacement in Frisco. Here's the before and after."</li>
<li><strong>25% seasonal/timely content:</strong> "Is your AC ready for the Phoenix summer? Schedule a tune-up before the heat hits."</li>
<li><strong>20% offers and promotions:</strong> "$500 off full HVAC system replacement. Limited time."</li>
<li><strong>15% educational content:</strong> "3 signs your water heater is about to fail."</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>What they don't post:</strong> Generic motivational quotes, irrelevant memes, or content that doesn't mention their service area.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Post at least twice per week. Use a simple content calendar: Monday (project update), Wednesday (educational tip), Friday (offer or seasonal reminder). Each post should include a photo, a location reference, and a clear call-to-action button.</p>
