<p><strong>To rank in the top 3, SABs needed significantly stronger signals elsewhere.</strong> The top-performing SABs in our sample averaged 312 reviews and 156 photos — far above the brick-and-mortar average.</p>
<h3>Why SABs Struggle</h3>
<p>Google's local algorithm relies heavily on <strong>proximity</strong> — the physical distance between the searcher and the business. For brick-and-mortar locations, this is straightforward: a pin on a map. For SABs with hidden addresses, Google must infer proximity from service area definitions, which are inherently less precise.</p>
<p>The Whitespark 2026 survey confirmed this shift: "Address showing on GBP" rose significantly in importance, while SABs reported increased difficulty ranking in competitive markets.</p>
<h3>How Top SABs Compensate</h3>
<p>The SABs that did rank in positions 1-3 shared these characteristics:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tightly defined service areas.</strong> They defined 8-15 specific cities rather than a 100-mile radius. One HVAC contractor in our sample listed 12 specific suburbs instead of "Dallas-Fort Worth metro."</li>
<li><strong>Location pages on their website.</strong> Every top SAB had dedicated pages for each major service area with unique content — not duplicate templates.</li>
<li><strong>Review location mentions.</strong> They encouraged customers to mention their city in reviews. A review saying "Great plumber in Richardson" reinforces geographic relevance.</li>
<li><strong>Local schema markup.</strong> Proper LocalBusiness schema with <code>areaServed</code> properties defined.</li>
<li><strong>Local citations with service area listed.</strong> Directories that allow service-area listings (Yelp, Angi, BBB) had consistent NAP + service area data.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>If you're a service-area business, don't just hide your address and hope for the best. Define specific service areas (cities, not radiuses). Build unique location pages. Encourage location-specific reviews. And accept that you may need stronger overall signals to compete with brick-and-mortar competitors.</p>
