<h3>The Dilution Problem</h3>
<p>We believe this pattern reflects Google's relevance scoring. Each secondary category adds potential relevance for related queries — but also dilutes the primary category's signal. A "Plumber" with 8 secondaries including "Landscaper" and "Painter" looks less like a focused plumbing business and more like a general handyman service.</p>
<p>Google's algorithm rewards specialization. A profile that clearly signals "we do this one thing exceptionally well" outranks a profile that signals "we do a little of everything."</p>
<h3>The Exception: Restoration</h3>
<p>Restoration contractors were the one trade where 4-5 secondary categories performed well. This makes sense: water damage, fire damage, and mold remediation are closely related services that customers often need simultaneously. A restoration company with multiple damage-type categories appears more comprehensive, not diluted.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>Select exactly 3 secondary categories that are closely related to your primary service. Remove any tangential categories. If you're a plumber with "Painter" or "Carpenter" as secondaries, delete them — they're hurting more than helping.</p>
