<h2>Key Finding #11: Q&amp;A Activity Showed No Measurable Correlation (And Now It's Gone)</h2>
<p><strong>The data:</strong> Q&amp;A count (questions + answers on the GBP) correlated at <strong>-0.06</strong> with ranking position — statistically meaningless.</p>
<p>This finding arrived just as Google began phasing out the Q&amp;A feature entirely. The Q&amp;A API was deprecated in November 2025, and Google is replacing manual Q&amp;A with AI-generated answers powered by Gemini.</p>
<h3>Why Q&amp;A Never Mattered</h3>
<p>Despite years of SEO blog posts recommending "seed your Q&amp;A section with common questions," our data found no relationship between Q&amp;A activity and ranking position. Profiles with 50+ Q&amp;A entries performed no better than profiles with zero entries.</p>
<p>This makes sense in retrospect. Q&amp;A content was never indexed in traditional search results. It appeared only on the Knowledge Panel, which relatively few users expanded. And Google never confirmed it as a ranking signal.</p>
<h3>What Replaces Q&amp;A</h3>
<p>Google's new "Ask this place" feature in Maps uses AI to generate answers from multiple sources:<br />
- Your website content<br />
- Your GBP description and services<br />
- Your reviews (and review responses)<br />
- Third-party mentions and citations</p>
<p>This shift makes your <strong>website FAQ content</strong> and <strong>review response keywords</strong> more important than ever. The Q&amp;A section you controlled is being replaced by AI-generated answers based on data you influence indirectly.</p>
<h3>The Action Item</h3>
<p>If you invested heavily in GBP Q&amp;A, that effort is now wasted. Redirect that energy to:<br />
1. Building a comprehensive FAQ page on your website with schema markup<br />
2. Writing keyword-rich review responses that mention services and locations<br />
3. Ensuring your website content clearly answers common customer questions</p>
