<h2>Your 30-Day GBP Optimization Action Plan</h2>
<p>Here's exactly what to do in the next 30 days, based on our findings.</p>
<h3>Week 1: Foundation Fixes</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Audit and correct your primary category</li>
<li>[ ] Select exactly 3 secondary categories; remove extras</li>
<li>[ ] Rewrite your business description (150-300 characters, keyword-rich)</li>
<li>[ ] Verify business hours are accurate; add after-hours if applicable</li>
<li>[ ] Tighten service area to specific cities (remove broad radius)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 2: Content Push</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Upload 20 photos (mix of before/after, team, equipment, projects)</li>
<li>[ ] Write and publish 2 GBP posts with photos and CTAs</li>
<li>[ ] Complete all available attributes in your GBP</li>
<li>[ ] Add products/services listings if not already present</li>
<li>[ ] Verify website link and appointment link work correctly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 3: Review System</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Set up automated review request (text + email) triggering 24 hours post-job</li>
<li>[ ] Respond to all existing unanswered reviews</li>
<li>[ ] Create a review response template that includes services and locations</li>
<li>[ ] Train technicians to mention review requests during job closeout</li>
<li>[ ] Add review request language to invoices and follow-up emails</li>
</ul>
<h3>Week 4: Ongoing Rhythm</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>[ ] Schedule 2 GBP posts per week in your calendar</li>
<li>[ ] Set a recurring task to upload 2-3 new photos weekly</li>
<li>[ ] Monitor review velocity — target 4-8 new reviews per month</li>
<li>[ ] Check "Ask this place" AI answers for your business; correct inaccuracies</li>
<li>[ ] Track ranking positions for your top 5 keywords weekly</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion / What to Do With This Data</h2>
<p>Local SEO isn't about doing everything. It's about doing the right things consistently.</p>
<p>Our analysis of 500 contractor Google Business Profiles reveals a clear hierarchy: category alignment and review recency matter most. Photos and posts create competitive separation. Profile completeness is table stakes. And some commonly recommended tactics — lengthy descriptions, Q&amp;A seeding, excessive secondary categories — show little to no correlation with rankings.</p>
<p>The contractors who win in local search aren't necessarily the ones with the biggest budgets. They're the ones who treat their GBP as a living asset — fresh reviews, regular posts, new photos, active engagement — rather than a one-time setup task.</p>
<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Review recency beats review count.</strong> A profile with 50 fresh reviews outranks one with 500 stale reviews.</li>
<li><strong>Primary category alignment is the #1 factor.</strong> Get this wrong and everything else is uphill.</li>
<li><strong>Photos are underrated.</strong> 60+ photos with weekly additions correlates strongly with top rankings.</li>
<li><strong>Post at least twice per week.</strong> The threshold from "inactive" to "active" is the biggest jump.</li>
<li><strong>SABs need stronger signals.</strong> Service-area businesses face structural disadvantages that require compensation through volume and precision.</li>
<li><strong>3 secondary categories is the sweet spot.</strong> More dilutes; fewer misses opportunities.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Your Next Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Today:</strong> Audit your primary category and secondary categories.</li>
<li><strong>This week:</strong> Set up automated review requests and upload your first batch of photos.</li>
<li><strong>This month:</strong> Establish a posting rhythm and respond to every existing review.</li>
<li><strong>This quarter:</strong> Track your ranking positions and adjust based on what moves.</li>
</ol>
<p>The data is clear. The tactics are known. The only variable left is execution.</p>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ Section</h2>
<h3>How many reviews do I need to rank in the top 3?</h3>
<p>There's no magic number. In our sample, position 1-3 profiles averaged 187 total reviews — but the range was 34 to 892. What matters more than total count is <strong>recency</strong>. A profile with 80 reviews and 3 new ones this month outperformed profiles with 400 reviews and none in the last 90 days. Target 4-8 new reviews per month consistently rather than chasing a specific total.</p>
<h3>Does hiding my address hurt my rankings?</h3>
<p>Our data suggests service-area businesses with hidden addresses rank in positions 1-3 <strong>31% less often</strong> than brick-and-mortar competitors with visible addresses — when other signals are equal. However, hiding your address is often required for compliance (if customers don't visit you). The solution: compensate with stronger signals elsewhere — more reviews, more photos, tighter service areas, and location-specific website content.</p>
<h3>How often should I post on my Google Business Profile?</h3>
<p>Post at least <strong>twice per week</strong>. Our data shows the biggest ranking jump happens between "never post" and "post 3-4 times per month." Profiles posting 5-8 times per month performed best overall. Posts expire after 7 days, so a profile without recent posts looks inactive to both Google and potential customers.</p>
<h3>What's the ideal number of photos for a contractor GBP?</h3>
<p>Aim for <strong>60+ photos</strong> to start, then add <strong>2-3 new photos weekly</strong>. In our sample, position 1 profiles averaged 127 photos. But the correlation is about ongoing freshness, not just total count. A profile that uploaded 50 photos in one day and stopped performed worse than a profile with 60 photos added consistently over six months.</p>
<h3>Should I respond to every review, even positive ones?</h3>
<p><strong>Yes.</strong> Profiles with 100% review response rates were 3.1x more likely to rank in positions 1-3. Responses aren't just customer service — they're keyword real estate. Mention your service and location naturally: "Thanks for trusting us with your Plano roof replacement." This reinforces relevance signals while building customer trust.</p>
<h3>How many secondary categories should I add?</h3>
<p><strong>Exactly 3.</strong> Our data shows a clear sweet spot: 3 secondary categories outperformed both 0 secondaries and 6+ secondaries. Adding too many dilutes your primary category signal. Choose secondary categories that are closely related to your primary service — not tangential offerings.</p>
<h3>Does my business description length matter for rankings?</h3>
<p><strong>No.</strong> We found no correlation between description length and ranking position. A 100-character description performed as well as a 750-character description. What matters is <strong>keyword presence</strong> — include your primary service and top 2-3 locations. Skip the lengthy origin story. Keep it under 300 characters.</p>
<h3>What happens if I'm closed when someone searches?</h3>
<p>Google's "openness" signal appears to act as a <strong>hard filter</strong> in 2026. In our sample, <strong>zero profiles</strong> ranked in positions 1-3 for searches conducted outside their stated hours. If you offer emergency or after-hours service, update your GBP hours to reflect it — even if you just have an answering service. Otherwise, you're invisible to the highest-intent searches.</p>
<h3>Do Google Business Profile posts really help rankings?</h3>
<p>Yes, indirectly. Post frequency correlated at -0.28 with ranking position in our data. Posts drive engagement (clicks, calls, directions) that feed behavioral signals. They also keep your profile looking active and current. The key is consistency — a steady rhythm of 2+ posts per week outperforms sporadic bulk posting.</p>
<h3>Is Q&amp;A still worth optimizing on my GBP?</h3>
<p><strong>No.</strong> Google is phasing out the Q&amp;A feature entirely, replacing it with AI-generated answers. Our data found no correlation between Q&amp;A activity and rankings even before the deprecation. Redirect your effort to building FAQ content on your website with schema markup, and writing keyword-rich review responses instead.</p>
<h3>How do ranking factors differ by trade?</h3>
<p>Significantly. Solar contractors need the strongest photo signals (-0.41 correlation). Restoration depends heavily on review recency (-0.46). Electrical showed the weakest correlations overall, likely due to lower competition. Benchmark against your specific trade, not generic "local business" advice.</p>
<h3>What's the fastest way to improve my local rankings?</h3>
<p>If you do nothing else, do these three things this week: (1) Verify your primary category exactly matches what customers search for, (2) Set up automated review requests to generate 4-8 fresh reviews per month, and (3) Upload 20 high-quality photos. These three actions alone address the highest-impact variables in our study.</p>
<h3>Can I rank well without a physical storefront?</h3>
<p>Yes, but it's harder. Service-area businesses in our sample needed stronger compensating signals — more reviews, more photos, tighter service area definitions, and location-specific website content. The SABs that ranked well treated their digital presence with the same precision a brick-and-mortar business brings to its physical location.</p>
<h3>How long until I see ranking improvements from GBP optimization?</h3>
<p>Based on our observations and industry consensus: category changes and hour updates can show impact within <strong>1-2 weeks</strong>. Review velocity improvements typically show within <strong>4-6 weeks</strong>. Photo and post frequency changes may take <strong>6-10 weeks</strong> to fully register. Local SEO is a compounding game — consistency over months beats intensity over days.</p>
<h3>Should I include keywords in my business name on GBP?</h3>
<p><strong>Be careful.</strong> While "keywords in business title" is a known ranking factor, Google actively penalizes businesses that add keywords to names that aren't part of their legal business name. This can trigger suspensions. If your legal name already includes a keyword (e.g., "Dallas Roofing Pros"), that's fine. If you're "Smith Construction" trying to become "Smith Construction Roofing Dallas," you're risking your profile.</p>
<h3>What's more important: review count or review rating?</h3>
<p><strong>Neither — review recency matters most.</strong> But if forced to choose between count and rating: a 4.6-star profile with 200 reviews outperformed a 5.0-star profile with 30 reviews in our sample. Perfect 5.0 ratings can actually trigger Google's fraud detection. Aim for 4.5-4.8 stars with steady volume.</p>
<h3>Do citations still matter for local rankings?</h3>
<p>Citations mattered less in our study than commonly assumed — but they're still foundational. Perfect NAP consistency across major directories is table stakes. What matters more for competitive differentiation is <strong>review velocity, photo freshness, and posting consistency</strong>. Get citations right, then move on to higher-impact activities.</p>
<h3>How do I know if my GBP is fully optimized?</h3>
<p>Use this checklist: Primary category aligned (yes/no), 3 secondary categories selected (yes/no), 60+ photos uploaded (yes/no), 2+ posts per week (yes/no), 100% review response rate (yes/no), business hours accurate including after-hours (yes/no), 150-300 character description with keywords (yes/no), all attributes completed (yes/no). Score 8/8 and you're in the top 10% of optimized profiles.</p>
